{"timestamp": "2026-02-04 19:53:54.966931", "event": "llm_start", "messages": "content='Based on the diagnosis report: ### Troubleshooting Diagnosis Report\\n\\n#### Network Description\\nThe network consists of:\\n- **Hosts**: `pc1` and `pc2`\\n- **Routers**: `router1` and `router2` (running FRRRouting)\\n- **Topology**:\\n  - `router1:eth0` connected to `router2:eth0`\\n  - `router1:eth1` connected to `pc1:eth0`\\n  - `router2:eth1` connected to `pc2:eth0`\\n\\n#### Observations\\n1. **Reachability Test Results**:\\n   - **Ping from `pc1` to `pc2`**:\\n     - All packets failed with the error: `Destination Net Unreachable`.\\n     - 100% packet loss observed.\\n   - **Ping from `pc2` to `pc1`**:\\n     - All packets failed with the error: `Destination Net Unreachable`.\\n     - 100% packet loss observed.\\n\\n   This indicates that neither host can communicate with the other, suggesting a potential routing or connectivity issue.\\n\\n2. **Routing Information**:\\n   - Attempts to retrieve the IP routing table from both `router1` and `router2` failed with the error: `/bin/bash: line 1: vtysh: command not found`.\\n   - This suggests that the FRRRouting service or its dependencies might not be properly configured or operational on both routers.\\n\\n3. **BGP Configuration**:\\n   - Attempts to retrieve the BGP configuration from both `router1` and `router2` also failed with the same error: `/bin/bash: line 1: vtysh: command not found`.\\n   - This further supports the possibility of misconfiguration or service issues with FRRRouting.\\n\\n#### Diagnosis\\n- **Anomalies Detected**:\\n  - Hosts `pc1` and `pc2` are unable to communicate, with all ping attempts resulting in `Destination Net Unreachable`.\\n  - Routing and BGP configuration retrieval from both routers failed, indicating potential issues with FRRRouting services.\\n\\n- **Localized Faults**:\\n  - The issue appears to be centered around the routers (`router1` and `router2`), as they are responsible for routing traffic between the hosts.\\n  - The inability to retrieve routing and BGP configurations suggests that the routers may not be functioning correctly.\\n\\n- **Potential Root Cause**:\\n  - The FRRRouting service on both routers might be misconfigured, disabled, or experiencing software-related issues.\\n  - Alternatively, there could be physical connectivity issues between the routers or between the routers and the hosts.\\n\\n#### Next Steps\\nFurther investigation is required to:\\n- Verify the operational status of FRRRouting services on both routers.\\n- Check the physical connectivity and interface configurations on the routers and hosts.\\n- Examine the network configuration and routing tables to identify any misconfigurations.\\n\\nThis concludes the diagnostic report., please provide the submission. Do not submit if no report available.' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={} id='a8b2b274-5390-4eab-80ca-58cce3923786'", "model": {"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "chat_models", "openai", "ChatOpenAI"], "kwargs": {"model_name": "kt-gpt-oss-rh014", "temperature": 0.0, "openai_api_key": {"lc": 1, "type": "secret", "id": ["OPENAI_API_KEY"]}, "openai_api_base": "https://dev.kora.kt.co.kr/v2", "request_timeout": 180.0, "max_retries": 2}, "name": "ChatOpenAI"}}
{"timestamp": "2026-02-04 19:54:05.214109", "event": "llm_end", "text": "**Solution Overview**\n\nThe root cause is that the two routers are not running FRR (the `vtysh` command is missing) and their interfaces have no IP configuration. Because of this the routers do not forward traffic between the two LANs, so `pc1` and `pc2` see *Destination Net Unreachable*.\n\nThe fix consists of three parts:\n\n1. **Install & start FRR on both routers**  \n2. **Configure the physical interfaces with IP addresses**  \n3. **Configure a simple BGP peering (or static routes) so that each router knows how to reach the opposite LAN**  \n\nAfter these steps the hosts will be able to ping each other.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Install & start FRR\n\n```bash\n# on router1 and router2\napt-get update\napt-get install -y frr frr-pythontools\n\n# enable the daemons we need (bgpd, zebra)\nsed -i 's/^bgpd=no/bgpd=yes/' /etc/frr/daemons\nsed -i 's/^zebra=no/zebra=yes/' /etc/frr/daemons\nsystemctl enable frr\nsystemctl start frr\n```\n\nNow the `vtysh` command is available.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interface IP configuration\n\n| Device   | Interface | IP address / mask | Description                |\n|----------|-----------|-------------------|----------------------------|\n| router1  | eth0      | 10.0.12.1/30      | link to router2            |\n| router1  | eth1      | 10.0.1.1/24       | LAN side (pc1)             |\n| router2  | eth0      | 10.0.12.2/30      | link to router1            |\n| router2  | eth1      | 10.0.2.1/24       | LAN side (pc2)             |\n| pc1      | eth0      | 10.0.1.2/24       | default gw 10.0.1.1        |\n| pc2      | eth0      | 10.0.2.2/24       | default gw 10.0.2.1        |\n\n### Commands (run on each host/router)\n\n```bash\n# router1\nip addr add 10.0.12.1/30 dev eth0\nip link set eth0 up\nip addr add 10.0.1.1/24 dev eth1\nip link set eth1 up\n\n# router2\nip addr add 10.0.12.2/30 dev eth0\nip link set eth0 up\nip addr add 10.0.2.1/24 dev eth1\nip link set eth1 up\n\n# pc1\nip addr add 10.0.1.2/24 dev eth0\nip link set eth0 up\nip route add default via 10.0.1.1\n\n# pc2\nip addr add 10.0.2.2/24 dev eth0\nip link set eth0 up\nip route add default via 10.0.2.1\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Routing – BGP (or static)\n\n### Option A – BGP (recommended for the lab)\n\n**router1 (AS 65001)**\n\n```bash\nvtysh -c \"configure terminal\" \\\n      -c \"router bgp 65001\" \\\n      -c \"bgp router-id 1.1.1.1\" \\\n      -c \"neighbor 10.0.12.2 remote-as 65002\" \\\n      -c \"neighbor 10.0.12.2 ebgp-multihop 2\" \\\n      -c \"neighbor 10.0.12.2 update-source eth0\" \\\n      -c \"network 10.0.1.0/24\" \\\n      -c \"exit\"\n```\n\n**router2 (AS 65002)**\n\n```bash\nvtysh -c \"configure terminal\" \\\n      -c \"router bgp 65002\" \\\n      -c \"bgp router-id 2.2.2.2\" \\\n      -c \"neighbor 10.0.12.1 remote-as 65001\" \\\n      -c \"neighbor 10.0.12.1 ebgp-multihop 2\" \\\n      -c \"neighbor 10.0.12.1 update-source eth0\" \\\n      -c \"network 10.0.2.0/24\" \\\n      -c \"exit\"\n```\n\nAfter the BGP session comes up (`show ip bgp summary`), each router will have a route to the opposite LAN and will forward traffic.\n\n### Option B – Static routes (simpler)\n\n```bash\n# router1\nvtysh -c \"configure terminal\" \\\n      -c \"ip route 10.0.2.0/24 10.0.12.2\"\n\n# router2\nvtysh -c \"configure terminal\" \\\n      -c \"ip route 10.0.1.0/24 10.0.12.1\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n```bash\n# From pc1\nping -c 4 10.0.2.2   # should succeed\n\n# From pc2\nping -c 4 10.0.1.2   # should succeed\n\n# On routers\nvtysh -c \"show ip route\"\nvtysh -c \"show ip bgp summary\"   # if BGP was used\n```\n\nAll pings must now receive replies, confirming that the network is correctly routed.\n\n---\n\n**Summary**\n\n1. Install and start FRR (`vtysh` becomes available).  \n2. Assign IP addresses to all router interfaces and host NICs.  \n3. Either enable BGP peering between the routers (advertising each LAN) **or** add a single static route on each router.  \n4. Verify connectivity with ping and routing table checks.\n\nAfter applying the above configuration the *Destination Net Unreachable* errors disappear and `pc1` ↔ `pc2` communication works as expected.", "generation_info": {"finish_reason": "stop", "logprobs": null}, "invalid_tool_calls": [], "usage_metadata": {"input_tokens": 750, "output_tokens": 1645, "total_tokens": 2395, "input_token_details": {}, "output_token_details": {}}}
